Z||000000||87ECO5||05-22-2001||03:05 PM||||I was going to bring my original "tracking" thread back but, since it's been archived, I can't update it.<P>I find it enjoyable to look back and see how my '87 economy coupe has progressed since I purchased it back in November/1999.<P>The coupe, still in its "original" form back in summer/2000:<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Scottscar.jpg"> <P>Then, later that fall:<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/87ECO5_360_wannabe_2_3.jpg"> <P>Finally, last month:<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fierrari1_1.jpg"> <P>The original DIS "Iron Duke" powerplant:<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Scottsmotor.jpg"> <P>Now, converted to run with a V6 (a remanufactured 3.4 installed by Ed Parks):<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fierrari3_1.jpg"> <P>Interesting to note (not so much to my wife though) - including the original purchase price of $2300, I've spent a grand total of $11,300 on this car.<P>You know what?  I'd have to say it's the best 12 grand I've ever spent on a car!||||reg||14
Z||000001||Formula88||05-22-2001||03:50 PM||||Sweet ride!  Much better deal than the $14000 I'm spending on my Grand Am!  Probably just as reliable, too, with the new engine.  I think I'm gonna sell the Grand Am and spend more money on Fieros!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000002||sqoach||05-22-2001||03:53 PM||||Wow! That is a slick car!<BR>Those wheels are nice, too.||||reg||14
Z||000003||ChadMan||05-22-2001||04:08 PM||||Looks great, Scott.  I guess there are two ways to look at this...<P>1)  You have sunk $11,300 into a car that has a real-world value of around $5000, and an insurance-book value of around $2000.  Boy, are you dumb.<P>2)  For $11,300, you have built a unique automobile that will run with new cars costing 2 to 4 times that amount.  That new engine should provide at least 125,000 miles of trouble-free service.  And it will never rust.  And it's a beautiful design in an ocean of lookalike, cookie-cutter cars.  And when/if it ever does need service, you know how to fix it and can turn to the PFF community for advice.  Boy, are you smart.<P>I choose viewpoint #2.  Congratulations on building a very special car.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Alex4mula||05-22-2001||04:16 PM||||Very nice looking car. And specially different. I like it. Good job!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phil||05-22-2001||05:19 PM||||If you're worried about the insurance value of your car- have it appraised and submit that figure to your insurance co. I called my co. and they suggested doing just that, if the time ever comes comes that I have a claim they will work from the appraised not the "book " value.||||reg||1
Z||000006||2M4 Dale||05-22-2001||11:41 PM||||WOW Scott...looks GREAT !!! I can't wait to see it in person.....You've done went and smoked my little 87 econo coupe......not to worry though...I'm not done with mine yet....just taking a working vacation from it. Oh and by the way.....I've got $6000 in my coupe so far....lol. I like Chads veiwpoint number 2......LMAO||||reg||14
Z||000007||AusFiero||05-23-2001||12:02 AM||||Looks great. A credit to yourself.<BR>Don't worry about the money you have the car you want. I have spent about $32,000 (about US$16,500) on my Fiero to date and know I couldn't sell it for what I have invested.<P>But on the upside I contacted my insurance company and took it for an inspection and they raised its value from $18,000 to $25,000, no questions asked as it was their appraised value. Plus they only put my premium up by $50. Most local Fieros have only been appraised at $12,000 to $18,000 they tell me so I am happy they think it is special compared to the others.<P>WHile on insurance, what do people pay around the globe for Fiero insurance ?<P>In Autralia with a 60% no claim bonus I pay AU$720 a year ($25,000 cover). That is for limited use, 4000kms a year and not to be used as a everyday go to work hack. Which suits me fine because I don't use it as a hack. $200 dearer for full everyday use insurance.<P>Just interested to see which country stings you most on insurance.<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero.gif"> <BR>When I was young I spent 80% of my money on Women, Gambling and Alcohol. I guess I wasted the rest.||||reg||1
Z||000008||88CoupeV6||05-25-2001||08:18 PM||||Looks great, I have not spent that much into my baby <B>yet</B> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">, but I have spent into the thousands. I only got my car for $400 and it seems every time I do something new to it, it costs more than $400 each time <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Oh well I love every wallet breaking moment of it.<P>------------------<BR>Josh <B>(known by TheFiero64 for first 1239 posts)</B><BR>     <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg">    <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||7
Z||000009||87ECO5||05-29-2001||07:33 AM||||Add another $550 to the bottom line.  I just added Mr. Mike's leather seats and Darrel Morse's strut tower brace over the holiday weekend.<P>You can make that number an even $600 if you count the high-speed buffer and accessories I purchased - and used over the weekend - to bring the paint back.  Unfortunately, the photo doesn't show the results well.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/car1.jpg">     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/seats3_jpg.jpg">     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/brace2_jpg.jpg"> ||||reg||14
